{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbJz1Qr1TvPcAR885EvH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I didn't compute a melting point for n-eicosane. The method fails the premise check, so I filed a refusal on the board instead of a number.\n\n**Why I refused**\n\n1. **The model is outside its domain.** MACE-MPA-0 was trained on inorganic crystal data from density functional theory (PBE), with no correction for dispersion (van der Waals) forces. Those forces are what hold an alkane crystal together and set its melting point. I ran a quick probe (`/work/scripts/probe.py`): two relaxed, straight C20H42 chains placed side by side.\n   - At 4.0 Å apart the model binds them by −2.63 eV (−254 kJ/mol).\n   - At 6.0 Å it is still −46 kJ/mol, and only reaches zero by about 9 Å.\n   - That is far too strong for a pair of chains. For scale, the measured heats of fusion for the C16 and C18 alkanes in `tier1.json` are about 53–60 kJ/mol per molecule.\n   - Any melting point from this model would come from that error, not from real physics.\n2. **The run is too expensive here.** A reliable melting point needs a simulated solid and liquid in contact: several thousand atoms over several nanoseconds, because long alkanes crystallise slowly. Simply heating a crystal until it melts overshoots the true value. I measured about 12 s per force evaluation for 3,968 atoms, so one nanosecond would take millions of steps, far beyond the available compute and budget.\n3. **Validation isn't possible.** The independent check would be to recover the measured melting points of n-hexadecane (291.0 K) or n-octadecane (301.0 K) from `tier1.json` with the same setup. The same two problems block that.\n\n**Limits of the probe:** I built the geometry by hand and tested only one side-by-side arrangement. I didn't run a quantum-chemistry reference. The evidence is diagnostic, not a benchmark, but the error is large enough to settle the question.\n\n**A version of the task that would work**\n- Use a model that handles dispersion: MACE-MPA-0 with a D3 dispersion correction, or a model trained on organic molecules such as MACE-OFF.\n- First check it against measured n-octadecane density and heat of fusion, and against the C16 and C18 melting points in `tier1.json`.\n- Then simulate solid and liquid in contact for C20 with at least 4,000 atoms for 2–5 ns at each temperature, using a neighbour list that runs on the GPU.\n- Report the uncertainty as the width of the temperature bracket where the interface stops moving, plus an estimate of system-size effects.\n\n**On the board**\n- Candidate `70beada5-04bb-4798-8d1a-3092ea819f2c` (n-eicosane, fulfils need #0)\n- Run report `cb941642-b51c-4c35-9dee-b97e5ef18aff` (status `refused`, with the full reasoning)\n\nI didn't post a `property_result`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1124,"cache_read_input_tokens":27608,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1124,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c88d9055-d07f-4406-922c-afa85268bf4e","uuid":"7cb03728-d48d-4930-9616-0e0507ab6dae","timestamp":"2026-11-01T01:06:04.324Z","request_id":"req_011CgbJz14GdvoQZjQ2gBDeA"}
